<.start_page current="interop">
  <section
    id="interop-lede"
    class="mb-12"
  >
    <h1 class="mb-3 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
      A payment that carries its own proof
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
      Any wallet can send it, any app can accept it, and anyone can check it without asking a company.
      The specs for that exist: BEEF, BUMP and BRC-100.
      This kit is a way to show they work together, with test vectors anyone can use and a check you can try by hand.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3">
      <.status kind="draft" />
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
      So far the vectors have been tried against one SDK, @bsv/sdk 2.4.0, in a one-off run, and against no live wallet.
      Nothing here says any wallet or project conforms to this kit or endorses it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section
    id="check"
    aria-labelledby="check-heading"
    class="mb-12"
  >
    <.strand_heading id="check-heading">
      Check a BEEF
    </.strand_heading>
    <p class="mb-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
      BEEF V1, BEEF V2 or Atomic BEEF around either, up to
      <span class="font-mono tabular-nums">{group(@max_bytes)}</span>
      bytes.
      Your bytes are read for this check and not kept.
    </p>

    <form
      id="interop-paste-form"
      phx-submit="check_paste"
      class="mb-6"
    >
      <label
        for="interop-beef"
        class="mb-1 block text-sm font-medium text-gray-900 dark:text-white"
      >
        BEEF as hex
      </label>
      <textarea
        id="interop-beef"
        name="beef"
        rows="6"
        spellcheck="false"
        autocomplete="off"
        class="w-full rounded-lg border-gray-300 bg-white font-mono text-xs text-gray-900 dark:border-gray-600 dark:bg-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100"
      ></textarea>
      <button
        id="interop-paste-submit"
        type="submit"
        phx-disable-with="Checking..."
        class="mt-2 rounded-lg bg-blue-600 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-blue-700"
      >
        Check this hex
      </button>
    </form>

    <form
      id="interop-upload-form"
      phx-change="validate_upload"
      phx-submit="check_upload"
    >
      <label
        for={@uploads.beef.ref}
        class="mb-1 block text-sm font-medium text-gray-900 dark:text-white"
      >
        Or a file, as raw bytes or hex
      </label>
      <.live_file_input
        upload={@uploads.beef}
        class="block w-full text-sm text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300"
      />
      <ul
        :if={@uploads.beef.entries != []}
        id="interop-upload-entries"
        role="list"
        class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300"
      >
        <li
          :for={entry <- @uploads.beef.entries}
          class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3"
        >
          <span class="break-all">{String.slice(entry.client_name, 0, 80)}</span>
          <span class="font-mono text-xs tabular-nums">{entry.progress}%</span>
          <button
            type="button"
            phx-click="cancel_upload"
            phx-value-ref={entry.ref}
            class="text-blue-700 underline hover:text-blue-900 dark:text-blue-400 dark:hover:text-blue-300"
          >
            Remove
          </button>
          <p
            :for={error <- upload_errors(@uploads.beef, entry)}
            class="w-full text-red-800 dark:text-red-200"
          >
            {upload_error(error)}
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p
        :for={error <- upload_errors(@uploads.beef)}
        class="mt-2 text-sm text-red-800 dark:text-red-200"
      >
        {upload_error(error)}
      </p>
      <button
        id="interop-upload-submit"
        type="submit"
        phx-disable-with="Checking..."
        class="mt-2 rounded-lg bg-blue-600 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-blue-700"
      >
        Check this file
      </button>
    </form>

    <div
      id="interop-result"
      aria-live="polite"
      class="mt-6"
    >
      <p
        :if={@error}
        id="interop-error"
        class="rounded-lg border border-red-200 bg-red-50 p-3 text-sm text-red-800 dark:border-red-800 dark:bg-red-900/30 dark:text-red-200"
      >
        {@error}
      </p>

      <div
        :if={@report}
        id="interop-report"
        class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-gray-700 dark:bg-gray-800"
      >
        <h3 class="mb-2 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
          The subject is
          <.verdict status={@report.status} />
        </h3>
        <p
          :if={@report.detail}
          class="mb-2 text-sm text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300"
        >
          {@report.detail}
        </p>
        <dl class="mb-4 grid grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-x-4 gap-y-1 text-sm">
          <dt class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">Format</dt>
          <dd class="font-mono text-xs text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">{@report.format}</dd>
          <dt class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">Subject</dt>
          <dd class="font-mono text-xs break-all text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">
            {@report.subject_txid}
          </dd>
          <dt
            :if={@report.confirmations}
            class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400"
          >
            Confirmations
          </dt>
          <dd
            :if={@report.confirmations}
            class="font-mono text-xs tabular-nums text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100"
          >
            {group(@report.confirmations)}
          </dd>
          <dt class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">Headers held to</dt>
          <dd class="font-mono text-xs tabular-nums text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">
            {if @report.indexed_tip_height, do: group(@report.indexed_tip_height), else: "none yet"}
          </dd>
        </dl>

        <p
          :if={@report.total > length(@report.rows)}
          id="interop-rows-capped"
          class="mb-2 text-sm text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300"
        >
          Showing {length(@report.rows)} of {group(@report.total)} transactions, the subject first.
        </p>
        <p
          :if={@report.unused_bumps > 0}
          id="interop-unused-bumps"
          class="mb-2 text-sm text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300"
        >
          {group(@report.unused_bumps)} BUMP(s) in this BEEF are used by no transaction.
        </p>

        <ul
          id="interop-rows"
          role="list"
          class="divide-y divide-gray-200 dark:divide-gray-700"
        >
          <li
            :for={row <- @report.rows}
            class="py-3"
          >
            <p class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-1">
              <.verdict status={row.status} />
              <span
                :if={row.subject}
                class="text-xs font-medium text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400"
              >
                Subject
              </span>
              <span
                :if={row.block_height}
                class="font-mono text-xs tabular-nums text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400"
              >
                Block {group(row.block_height)}<span :if={row.index}>, position {group(row.index)}</span>
              </span>
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-xs break-all text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">
              {row.txid}
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              {row.detail}
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="mt-3 space-y-2 border-t border-gray-200 pt-3 text-sm text-gray-700 dark:border-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          <p>
            {@report.claim}
          </p>
          <p>
            {@report.trust}
          </p>
          <p>
            Not checked:
            <span
              :for={{name, index} <- Enum.with_index(@report.not_checked)}
            >{if index > 0, do: ", "}{not_checked_label(name)}</span>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section
    id="claims"
    aria-labelledby="claims-heading"
    class="mb-12"
  >
    <.strand_heading id="claims-heading">
      What this check says, and what it leaves to you
    </.strand_heading>
    <div class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
      <p>
        The check says whether a transaction's bytes sit under a merkle root in a block header.
        That is inclusion in a block, and it's the whole claim.
      </p>
      <p>
        It doesn't run scripts or check signatures, so it can't say a transaction is valid.
        It doesn't look for double spends, so it can't say inputs were unspent.
        It doesn't read amounts or terms, so it can't say the right payment was made.
        A receiver does those parts, and each one is listed below.
      </p>
      <p>
        Roots are compared with the headers Bitblocks holds, which come from one node.
        Keep your own headers if you'd rather not take ours on trust.
      </p>
    </div>
    <dl class="mt-4 divide-y divide-gray-200 border-y border-gray-200 text-sm dark:divide-gray-700 dark:border-gray-700">
      <div class="py-3">
        <dt>
          <.verdict status={:proven} />
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          Its bytes hash to its txid, its BUMP climbs to a root, and that root is the one in a verified header on the chain we hold.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="py-3">
        <dt>
          <.verdict status={:anchored} />
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          It has no BUMP, and every input leads, within this BEEF, to proven transactions.
          It is not itself shown to be in any block.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="py-3">
        <dt>
          <.verdict status={:unknown} />
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          We can't say: we hold no usable header at that height, or the BEEF lacks what it needs.
          Never read this as a pass.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="py-3">
        <dt>
          <.verdict status={:failed} />
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          The evidence is wrong: the root differs from our header, the BUMP reaches no root or can't be that block's, or an ancestor failed.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section
    id="vectors"
    aria-labelledby="vectors-heading"
    class="mb-12"
  >
    <.strand_heading id="vectors-heading">
      Test vectors, open to everyone
    </.strand_heading>
    <div class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
      <p>
        <a
          id="interop-vectors-link"
          href="/interop/vectors/v1.json"
          class="font-mono text-xs underline text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 dark:text-blue-400 dark:hover:text-blue-300"
        >/interop/vectors/v1.json</a>
        needs no key and no account.
        Version <span class="font-mono tabular-nums">{@vectors.version}</span>
        holds <span class="font-mono tabular-nums">{@vectors.total}</span>
        vectors: <span class="font-mono tabular-nums">{@vectors.valid}</span>
        valid and <span class="font-mono tabular-nums">{@vectors.invalid}</span>
        deliberately invalid.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each one gives its bytes, the outcome to expect and where it came from, and the file carries the headers to check against.
        Examples from BRC-62 and BRC-74 sit beside bytes written by @bsv/sdk, a real mainnet transaction, and cases we crafted to fail.
      </p>
      <p>
        A test runs every vector through our reader and verifier, so the file can't drift from what the code does.
        Where we're stricter than a spec, the vector is marked <code class="font-mono text-xs">hardening</code>, and a reader can differ there and still follow the spec.
      </p>
      <.code_panel
        label="Read the first vector"
        code={vectors_example()}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section
    id="sender"
    aria-labelledby="sender-heading"
    class="mb-12"
  >
    <.strand_heading id="sender-heading">
      What a conforming sender does
    </.strand_heading>
    <ul
      role="list"
      class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300"
    >
      <li>
        Sends the payment as Atomic BEEF (BRC-95), with the payment as the subject.
      </li>
      <li>
        Includes every unconfirmed ancestor, back to transactions with a BUMP (BRC-74).
      </li>
      <li>
        Puts parents ahead of children, and ends with the payment (BRC-62).
      </li>
      <li>
        Leaves out anything outside the subject's ancestry.
      </li>
      <li>
        Writes each VarInt in its shortest form, with nothing after the end.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section
    id="receiver"
    aria-labelledby="receiver-heading"
    class="mb-12"
  >
    <.strand_heading id="receiver-heading">
      What a conforming receiver does
    </.strand_heading>
    <ul
      role="list"
      class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300"
    >
      <li>
        Reads the bytes strictly, and refuses anything that doesn't parse.
      </li>
      <li>
        Checks every BUMP's root against headers it trusts, by its own choice of source.
      </li>
      <li>
        Treats unknown as not yet, and failed as no.
      </li>
      <li>
        Runs the payment's scripts against the outputs it spends, and adds up the amounts.
      </li>
      <li>
        Confirms the outputs pay what was agreed, to whom it was agreed.
      </li>
      <li>
        Sends the payment to the network, and keeps the BEEF with its record.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section
    id="pattern"
    aria-labelledby="pattern-heading"
    class="mb-12"
  >
    <.strand_heading id="pattern-heading">
      One payment, end to end
    </.strand_heading>
    <ol
      role="list"
      class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300"
    >
      <li>
        The receiver states the terms: the amount, and the script to pay.
      </li>
      <li>
        The wallet builds the payment and produces Atomic BEEF.
        A BRC-100 wallet returns this from <code class="font-mono text-xs">createAction</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        The wallet hands the bytes straight to the receiver, with no third party in between.
      </li>
      <li>
        The receiver verifies against headers: the payment reads anchored, its parents proven.
      </li>
      <li>
        The receiver checks scripts, amounts and terms, then sends the payment to the network.
      </li>
      <li>
        The receiver gives back a receipt: the txid, a hash of the BEEF, the status, the height of the headers it used, and the time.
      </li>
      <li>
        Once the payment is mined, the receiver fetches its BUMP, checks again, and the payment reads proven.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
      The receipt's shape is our suggestion, and no standard defines it yet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section
    id="anchored-proven"
    aria-labelledby="anchored-proven-heading"
    class="mb-12"
  >
    <.strand_heading id="anchored-proven-heading">
      Anchored and proven, for a fresh payment
    </.strand_heading>
    <div class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
      <p>
        A fresh payment isn't in a block yet, so anchored is the best it can read, and that's the normal result.
        It means the coins trace, inside this BEEF, to transactions that are in blocks.
      </p>
      <p>
        Anchored doesn't mean accepted.
        The scripts may not pass, an input may already be spent, and the network hasn't said anything yet.
        It's the start of a receiver's work, and for a small payment that work may be enough to go ahead.
        How much risk to take before proven is the receiver's call.
      </p>
      <p>
        Proven means the payment itself sits in a block under a header you rely on.
        That is the strongest thing this check can say, and it still covers inclusion alone.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section
    id="gaps"
    aria-labelledby="gaps-heading"
    class="mb-12"
  >
    <.strand_heading id="gaps-heading">
      Known gaps
    </.strand_heading>
    <ul
      role="list"
      class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300"
    >
      <li>
        Scripts, signatures, amounts and double spends aren't checked here, so an anchored transaction may still be invalid.
        An input's output index isn't looked at either.
      </li>
      <li>
        Headers come from one node, and we trust it for which chain is longest.
      </li>
      <li>
        A block's transaction count isn't in its header, so the check on a proof's shape rests on our node's word.
      </li>
      <li>
        BRC-95 doesn't say which byte order the subject txid takes.
        We read internal order, as the SDK writes it.
      </li>
      <li>
        BRC-96 treats a txid-only entry as agreed between two parties.
        A third-party check has no such agreement, so we answer unknown.
      </li>
      <li>
        No live wallet has been tried yet, and one SDK run is a start rather than a result.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
      If you build wallets or apps, run the vectors and tell us where they're wrong.
      The full write-up is in
      <.text_link href="https://github.com/afomi/bitblocks/blob/develop/docs/INTEROP_KIT.md">docs/INTEROP_KIT.md</.text_link>.
    </p>
  </section>
</.start_page>
